From 61f5b56967022462430d776da631622959145bd8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:50:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GBM: the luminance that the HDR10 InfoFrame tells the
 display, limited (for a projector)

A projector, or a display that adapts its picture to the static values
of HDR10, maps less of a picture away when it is told a lower peak:
the "HDR10 limiter" of avdvplus (its settings and notes read as data),
asked for by owners of projectors (cinema-ONE, a JVC NZ9, among them).

System > Display, at the expert level: "HDR10: limit the luminance told
to the display" with "HDR10: the luminance told at most" (100 to 4000
cd/m2): the peak of the mastering display, MaxCLL and MaxFALL that go
in HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA are at most that value (0, not given, stays 0).
The picture sent does not change, only what is said of it; the dynamic
metadata of HDR10+ go as they are. Off by default: the values of the
video are those the master was made with. kodi.log says once, for each
set of values, what the video had and what the display is told.
English and Spanish.

Adapting HDR10 and HDR10+ to the display in the player, only where the
master is brighter than it, is a larger change (a mapping in the
ordinary renderer of Kodi, whose cost on an integrated GPU is not
measured) and is not in this commit.
